the modern warfare series has always  been about the messiness of modern war  the fundamentally different rules of  engagement that come with a battle that  has no set battlefields when the fight  could be anywhere at any time where do  you draw the line between doing what's  right and doing what has to be done  throughout call of duty modern warfare  campaign that line is chemical weapons  it's a safe line to draw people are  largely in agreement that chemical  weapons are beyond horrific but there  are other horrors of war some of which  modern warfare depicts starkly and  strong but uncomfortable missions just  when it could really make a point about  any other aspect of modern war it pulls  back  modern warfare makes old observations  and presents them with new flourishes  those new flourishes do make for a good  campaign and solid multiplayer but is  when modern warfare asks you to think  harder that it falls short  in one of the game's most distressing  levels you play as Farah  a young girl in a fictional war-torn  Middle Eastern country as she hides from  both a Russian terrorist and the deadly  gas his cohorts have been leashed on her  town to escape you have to kill a man  twice your size with his own gun  it's a deeply uncomfortable experience  but the flashback serves to illustrate  why Farah now the leader of a group of  freedom fighters refuses to use chemical  weapons or associate with anyone who  does it is a hard line she won't cross  even though she's had to face a lot of  ugliness in the course of defending her  country in many ways Farah is Modern  Warfare's moral compass there are a few  key players in Modern Warfare's proxy  war and everyone you play as sergeant  Kyle Garrick from the UK rogue American  soldier Alex echo 3-1 and sometimes  Farah herself abides by her one rule  outside of that though the rules are  much murkier in getting pulled into a  war between the Russian terrorists a  separatist group from Forrest country  and the freedom fighters themselves US  and UK military personnel disagree on  how to best proceed with the situation  matters of disobeying orders sacrificing  some life to save others taking civilian  hostages and even torture and on these  matters the moral compass is Captain  Price  a returning face from the original  modern warfare and undeniably a  problematic fave captain price is the  seasoned badass who takes the lead in  most Garrick missions early levels with  price are among the best they range from  large-scale high-octane firefights to a  carefully planned raid on a terrorist  hideout with less than a dozen enemies  total price guides you through the  different approaches you need for each  mission and his mentorship both in the  mechanical skills you need to be  successful and the hard choices you have  to make along the way makes these  missions memorable while Alex's missions  don't stick out quite as much in a  gameplay sense he gets a sniping level  reminiscent of the original Modern  Warfare's all Gallade up but with more  enemies and otherwise a few cool gadgets  his dynamic was farah is strong though  he follows far as lead on her turf and  on her terms because he believes in the  cause and they share mutual respect it's  disappointing though that Farah doesn't  play more of a role while she is a key  part of Alex's missions and the driving  force behind much of the story he only  plays her a few times on top of the  childhood flashback there is an even  more disturbing flashback later on in  which you see the full extent of fires  resolve experiencing her suffering this  way borders on unnecessary and it's  already established in Alex's missions  that she's a respected leader and a  strong-willed person in general while I  liked Alex I would have rather just  played as Farah in those missions then  get to know her character largely  through her trauma while modern warfare  raises difficult questions in the end it  fails to interrogate them further all  you get is a vague we all did what we  had to do sentiment rather than anything  more substantial or interesting the  ending isn't bad but it is a safe one  leaving you to think on the harder  questions for yourself why if we got our  hands tied  just take the bloody gloves off a fight  if anything modernwarfare let's fara  down with the bizarre and much-discussed  inclusion of white phosphorus as a  killstreak in multiplayer given how  strong the campaign's emphasis is on  chemical weapons being a reprehensible  war crime it's tone-deaf to include one  in multiplayer  [Music]  outside of any somatic contradictions  Modern Warfare's multiplayer is up to  par with a variety of game types for  different kinds of players realism mode  is undeniably the highlight it's  somewhere between the familiar core and  hardcore modes bridging the gap between  them oddly enough you can take more  damage than in hardcore and your health  regenerates like a dozen core but  realism removes the hood entirely going  beyond hardcore to strip out the kill  feed on top of everything else in order  to confirm a kill you have to listen for  the sound effect that plays upon death  it's a fantastic balance for those who  want more of a chance to survive a scrap  rather than dying in one or two shots  like in hardcore but with the rest of  the challenge intact it's a smart  satisfying evolution and there's a  stubborn hardcore only player it's one I  could see myself playing exclusively  going forward while none of the new game  types are earth-shattering some are  better editions than others TDM 2810 v10  version of the classic six v6 team  deathmatch mode is the least inventive  or warranted of them instead functioning  as a more bloated version of regular TDM  with bigger maps that can make getting  back into the action in overly long  process one of the two maps I've tried  Euphrates bridge also suffers from  balance problems on top of that of the  two spawns one is much closer to the  bridge dividing the map and the closer  side was almost guaranteed victory in  every match I played gunfight is the  antithesis of TDM 20 it's a 1 life 2 V 2  mode in which your loadout rotates each  round and the goal is to kill your two  opponents with the means available to  you before they get you first with a  relatively level playing field battles  are often exhilaratingly close and it's  hard to get discouraged by a loss since  rounds go by so quickly  ground war is somewhere in the middle  maps are sprawling with five control  points to capture in one safe zone for  each team on either end  unlike in TDM 20 you can pretty easily  get back to the fight after dying by  responding at any capture point your  team owns or on two vehicles for your  teammates provided they're not actively  in a fight  having objective points is also helpful  for keeping such a large game type more  structured than the free-for-all of TDM  that said matches can drag on a bit too  long as there isn't anything to break up  the constant tug-of-war for capture  points as of this writing spec UPS is  the mode I've had the least experience  with though it's not one I particularly  want to play much more of on paper it's  a co-op mode where you and a team  complete a set of objectives and are  rewarded with some story in practice my  team of four could fairly complete a  handful of the objectives on both of the  missions we attempted this was largely  due to frustrating enemy spawning  enemies seem to generate endlessly from  all directions and it's all too easy to  get overwhelmed by them to add insult to  injury there are also no clear waves  it's just enemies from everywhere at all  times we tried a few different  approaches on each of the two missions  to try to figure it out but no matter  what we did it didn't help our  understanding of the mode itself it's  just frustrating Lee inexplicably hard  that said I will be trying it again in  the coming days to see if there was  anything we were missing and I also have  to play the ps4 exclusive survival mode  the pitfalls of spec ops don't detract  from what modern warfare does well  realism mode is an excellent addition to  the slate and although not all the new  multiplayer modes are great gunfight is  a refreshing standout and while the  campaign ends up playing it safe in the  end it's still a memorable one and it  lays a strong foundation for where the  modern warfare series could go from here  and you keeping your word on I don't do  this to keep my word  [Music]  you
 

